I cannot confirm that, given experience from several companies. It is
surely _not_ the default shell on all the commercial Unix systems, it's
very likely the default shell on Linux. And I've never encountered an
admin who asked me what shell I want; you'll get the company standard
(and if you have some basic Unix knowledge change your shell yourself).
You'll find tcsh (because it is "a superior interactive shell"), you'll
find ksh (because "the IT policy says to use the same standard shell on
Linux as used on the commercial Unixes", and even csh (just because the
sysadmin came from an ancient BSD company and IT management doesn't care
or doesn't know better). Whatever you consider a "normal admin" to be.

> 3) For Joe Random User who doesn't know what shell he has, the shell
